\label{twodoublebranch}
The four-fold branched covering of Proposition \ref{80tori} factors as the composition of two double branched coverings 
\begin{equation*}
M\stackrel{p_1}{\longrightarrow} N\stackrel{p_2}{\longrightarrow} \Sigma\times\Sigma
\end{equation*}
each only having simple singularities. In particular, the ramification loci of both $p_1$ and $p_2$ are a collection of disjoint embedded surfaces, and in the case of $p_2$ they are a subset the ramification locus of $p$ consisting of $40$ disjoint tori.

\label{coho}
Let $(Y,\omega_Y)$ be a closed symplectic $4$-manifold, and consider oriented embedded Lagrangian tori $\{T_i\}$ in $Y$ with the following properties:
\begin{enumerate}
\item the $T_i$ intersect each other transversely in at most one point;
\item there is a cohomology class $[\eta]\in H^2(Y;\mathbb{R})$ such that $\langle[\eta],[T_i]\rangle>0$ for all $i$. 
\end{enumerate}
  Then there is a symplectic form $\omega'_Y$ (arbitrarily close to $\omega_Y$) such that all the $T_i$ are symplectic.

Consider a representative closed $2$-form $\eta$ for the cohomology class in assumption (2). Choose an area form $\omega_i\in \Omega^2(T_i)$ on each $T_i$ such that
\begin{equation*}
\int_{T_i}\eta=\int_{T_i}\omega_i>0,
\end{equation*}
so that one can find $\alpha_i\in \Omega^1(T_i)$ with
\begin{equation*}
d\alpha_i=\omega_i-\eta\lvert_{T_i}\in  \Omega^2(T_i).
\end{equation*}
We will also interpret $\alpha_i$ as a form on $\nu T_i$ via pull-back. Finally choose compactly supported functions
\begin{equation*}
f_i:\Delta_i\rightarrow \mathbb{R}
\end{equation*}
with $f_i(0)=1$, which we also interpret as a function on $\nu T_i$ by pull-back. We will consider the $1$-forms with compact support in $\nu T_i$ given by
\begin{equation*}
 \beta_i=f_i\alpha_i\in\Omega^1_c(\nu T_i)\subset \Omega^1(Y).    
\end{equation*}
In the local coordinates introduced above,  $\alpha_i$ lies in $\mathrm{span}\{dy_1,dy_2\}$, while $df_i$ lies in $\mathrm{span}\{dx_1,dx_2\}$ at every point.
Because of this, we see that when taking the exterior derivative
\begin{equation*}
d\beta_i=df_i\wedge \alpha_i+f_i\wedge d\alpha_i\in \Omega^2(Y)
\end{equation*}
the first summand restricts to zero on each $T_j$ and therefore
\begin{equation*}
d\beta_i\lvert_{T_j}=
\begin{cases}
\omega_i-\eta\lvert_{T_i}\text{ if }i=j\\
0 \text{ otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\end{equation*}
Because of this, the closed two-form
$$\eta'=\eta+\sum d\beta_i$$
restricts to the area form $\omega_i$ on each $T_i$, and for $\varepsilon>0$ sufficiently small the form 
\begin{equation*}
\omega'_Y=\omega_Y+\varepsilon\eta'\in\Omega^2(Y)
\end{equation*}
is a new symplectic form on $Y$ (being symplectic is an open condition) for which the $T_i$ are symplectic surfaces.

This follows from \cite[Lemma 2.3]{Gom} (which deals with a much more general situation) and its proof; in fact, we can arrange that the isotopy is supported in a neighborhood of each of the transverse intersections, with one of the two intersecting surfaces remaining fixed.
\par
This result is the differential version of the following linear algebra observation (which provides the pointwise model at the intersection point). Consider in $(\mathbb{C}^2,\omega_{std})$ a two-dimensional symplectic vector subspace $V_0$ which is positively transverse to $\mathbb{C}\times\{0\}$ (equipped with its natural complex orientation). By (positive) transversality, we can write (identifying each summand of $\mathbb{C}^2=\mathbb{C}\oplus\mathbb{C}$ with $\mathbb{R}^2$ with standard basis $\{e_1,e_2\}$) considering oriented spans throughout
\begin{equation*}
V_0=\mathrm{span}\{v_1\oplus e_1, v_2\oplus e_2\}\subset  \mathbb{C}\oplus\mathbb{C}
\end{equation*}
for some vectors $v_1,v_2\in\mathbb {{R}}^2$. The fact that $V_0$ is a positive symplectic subspace implies that (denoting by $\omega_0$ the standard form on $\mathbb{R}^2$, so that $\omega_{std}=\omega_0\oplus\omega_0$):
\begin{equation*}
0<\omega_{std}(v_1\oplus e_1, v_2\oplus e_2)=\omega_0(v_1,v_2)+1.  
\end{equation*}
From this, we see that because
\begin{equation*}
t^2\omega_0(v_1,v_2)+1>0\text{ for all }t\in[0,1],
\end{equation*}
the one parameter family of subspaces
\begin{equation*}
V_t=\mathrm{span}\{(tv_1)\oplus e_1, (tv_2)\oplus e_2\}
\end{equation*}
forms for $t\in[0,1]$ a path of symplectic subspaces positively transverse to $\mathbb{C}\times\{0\}$ connecting $V_1=V$ to $V_0=\{0\}\times\mathbb{C}$.
\par
As explained in detail in the proof of \cite[Lemma 2.3]{Gom}, from this one constructs a smooth isotopy $\varphi_t$ for $t\in[0,1]$ such that $\varphi_0=\mathrm{Id}$, for all $t\in[0,1]$ the $\{\varphi_t(T_i)\}$ form a collection of positively transverse $\omega_Y'$-symplectic tori, and the $\{\varphi_1(T_i)\}$ they are furthermore $\omega'_Y$-orthogonal. The claim of the Lemma then follows by applying the isotopy to the symplectic form instead of the Lagrangians.

Note that in general two symplectic subspaces can be \textit{negatively} transverse. In the notation of the proof above can take for example
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{span}(e_1\oplus 0, e_2\oplus 0)\text{ and } \mathrm{span}(v_1\oplus -e_1, v_2\oplus e_2)
\end{equation*}
with $\omega_0(v_1,v_2)>1$. On the other hand, orthogonal symplectic subspaces are necessarily positively intersecting, so that the condition in the lemma is necessary.

\label{normal}
In the situation above, we can identify a neighborhood of
\begin{equation*}
\bigcup_i T_i\subset (Y,\omega''_Y)
\end{equation*}
with a corresponding plumbing of the product symplectic manifolds $(D(r_i)\times T_i, \omega_0\oplus \omega''_Y\lvert_{T_i})$ (for some choice of $\{r_i\}$).

Because of the work we have done regarding local models, and in particular Corollary \ref{normal}, we can focus on the simple case of the branched cover between neighborhoods of the origin in $\mathbb{C}^2$ (equipped with the standard symplectic form $\omega_{std}$) given by
\begin{equation*}
f(z_1,z_2)=(z_1^2,z_2).
\end{equation*}
In this model the {preimage of the} ramification locus is given by 
\begin{equation*}
\{z_1=0\}=\{0\}\times \mathbb{C}\quad(=R),
\end{equation*}
while its symplectic orthogonal (which is the model for the preimage remaining non-branching $T_i$, near the intersection points) is given by
\begin{equation}\label{wantsymp}
V:=\{z_2=0\}=\mathbb{C}\times \{0\}=\mathrm{ker}df_{(0,0)}\quad (=f^{-1}(T^{nr})).
\end{equation}
One readily computes that in these coordinates ($z_j=x_j+iy_j$, $j=1,2$)
\begin{equation*}
f^*\omega_{std}=4(x_1^2+y_1^2)\cdot dx_1\wedge dy_1+dx_2\wedge dy_2,
\end{equation*}
so that it vanishes on $V$ at the origin. In order to remedy this we will adjust it by exact forms defined in the local models  so that we get a symplectic form on $\mathbb{C}^2$ for which $V$ is a symplectic submanifold.
\par
Given $p\in R$, looking at the local models consider the $2$-form
\begin{equation*}
\alpha_p=d\left[\chi_1(|z_1|)\chi_2(|z_2|) x_1dy_1\right]
\end{equation*}
where
\begin{equation*}
\chi_i:\mathbb{R}^{\geq0}\rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{\geq0}
\end{equation*}
are smooth non-increasing compactly supported functions which are $1$ near $0$, the specific shape of which is to be decided. Explicitly, $\alpha_p$ involves three terms of which only two only involve $dx_1\wedge dy_1$, namely
\begin{equation*}
\chi_2(|z_2|)\chi_1(|z_1|)\cdot dx_1\wedge dy_1\text{ and }x_1\chi_2(|z_2|)\cdot d\chi_1(|z_1|)\wedge dy_1
\end{equation*}
Note that the first form coincides with $dx_1\wedge dy_1$ in a neighborhood of $p$, while the latter vanishes identically in a neighborhood of $p$. Therefore by compactness, summing the forms corresponding to a suitable finite set of $R$ (and for suitable choices of the $\chi_i$), we obtain a closed $2$-form $\alpha$ on $X$ with the property that $f^*\omega_Y+\varepsilon \alpha$ is readily checked to be a symplectic form on $X$ for which the preimages of the non-branching tori is symplectic.

Such a class $[\eta]$ exists if and only there exists an open half space containing all classes $\{[T_i]\}\subset H_2(\Sigma\times \Sigma;\mathbb{R})$, or, equivalently, there is no non-trivial linear combination
\begin{equation*}
\lambda_{i_1}[T_{i_1}]+\dots+\lambda_{i_k}[T_{i_k}]=0
\end{equation*}
with all coefficients positive. Indeed, such a linear combination cannot exists, because by Proposition \ref{80tori} there is a class $[T_j]$ such that $[T_j]\cdot [T_{i_1}]=1$ (and $[T_j]\cdot [T_{i_r}]\geq 0$ for $r=1\dots k$), so that taking intersections one obtains $\lambda_{i_1}=0$.
